      "sha": "2fe46a92d9a9acd0b656daae92c7a0a28c824bd9",
      "author": "matiasba",
      "summary": "Decode video in software on NVIDIA/Linux (#1682)",
      "description": "On Linux with the proprietary NVIDIA driver, libVLC picks the nvdec\ndecoder and pairs it with the glinterop_nvdec OpenGL interop. VLC itself\nonly supports that interop on its own GLX output: display.c forces\ngl_name to \"glx\" for the NVDEC chromas, but only when the window type is\nVLC_WINDOW_TYPE_XID. The Unity plugin renders through the external\n\"wextern\" window and \"vgl\" output, so that guard never applies and VLC\nhappily selects an interop the context cannot handle.\n\nIt fails silently. The plugin keeps reporting new frames, rotates its\ntexture pointers and the blit into the output RenderTexture runs at the\nusual rate, but the texture contents never change -- the video shows its\nfirst frame and then appears frozen while playback, timing and looping\nall keep working normally.\n\nReading back the output texture makes it obvious: the hash of its pixels\nchanges once and then stays constant for the rest of the song.\n\nThe plugin's other Linux paths do not help. Forcing the DMA-BUF fallback\nneeds the DRI3 extension, which is missing under XWayland, and the EGL\nbackend renders nothing at all. Restricting the decoder list is what\nworks, so do that on NVIDIA only and leave hardware decoding alone\neverywhere else.\n\nCo-authored-by: Matias Barros <matias.barros@invgate.com>"
    },
